course: biology
lesson: 18
title: The lion: why people call it «king of the beasts»; prides and roars
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: People have called the lion king of the beasts for thousands of years: the big golden male with his mane, the roar that rolls across the plain at dawn, and the pride that lies in the open without bothering to hide.
explain: That royal lion fills our world: statues in city squares, three lions on the England shirt, the lion in Aesop's fables, Simba on Pride Rock, and Richard the Lionheart.
explain: But the poster saying king of the jungle is wrong: lions live on savanna, grassland and open woodland in Africa, and one small group in the dry Gir Forest of India, not in jungles.
explain: Real lion life is less royal and more teamwork: lionesses do most of the hunting together, the males guard the land, and everyone sleeps up to about 20 hours a day.
explain: The real king is in trouble: wild lions have lost most of the land they once roamed across Africa, southern Europe and Asia.
fact: Only male lions grow manes; a mane makes a lion look bigger and protects his neck in fights, and a dark full mane shows he is healthy, though in very hot places some males have thin manes or none.
fact: A lion's roar can be heard about 8 kilometres away; lions roar to mark their territory and call their pride, mostly at night and dawn, when the air is cool and still and sound carries best.
fact: Lions have a special voice box with square-shaped folds that make a very deep, loud sound.
fact: Lions do not live in jungles but in African savanna, grassland and open woodland, and in the Gir Forest, a dry forest in India; king of the beasts is the better old name.
fact: Lionesses do most of the hunting as a team, some driving the prey and others waiting in ambush; they are lighter and faster than males, while males hunt big prey like buffalo and mostly guard the pride's land.
fact: Lions are the only truly social cats; a pride is related females, their cubs and a few males, and together they hunt big prey, guard cubs and defend their land.
fact: The lion is on many coats of arms and flags, like England's three lions, Scotland's red lion and Sri Lanka's flag, as a symbol of courage and strength; King Richard I was called the Lionheart for his bravery.
fact: In Aesop's fable from ancient Greece, about 2500 years ago, a little mouse saves a lion by gnawing through a net.
fact: In The Lion King, Simba is a young lion who will become king; simba means lion in Swahili, a language of East Africa.
fact: Lions rest or sleep up to about 20 hours a day to save energy for hunting, and a lion can eat 30 kilograms or more at one meal and then not eat for days.
fact: Lion cubs are born with spotty coats that help hide them in the grass, and lionesses in a pride often raise cubs together, letting cubs drink from other mothers.
fact: Lions are the only cats with a tuft at the end of the tail, which may help the pride follow each other in long grass; some have a small hard spur hidden in it, and nobody knows what it is for.
fact: Wild lions now live mainly in parts of Africa, and Asiatic lions only in and around Gir Forest in India, where a 2025 count was reported at about 890; lions lose land to farms and are killed when they attack cattle.
exercise: Which lions grow a mane? | Only the males | Only the females | Both males and females | Only the cubs
exercise: What does a mane do for a male lion? | Makes him look bigger and protects his neck in fights | Keeps the sun out of his eyes | Helps him swim and float across deep, fast rivers | Hides him in long grass
exercise: How far can a lion's roar be heard? | About 8 kilometres | Only about 80 metres | About 80 kilometres | Only inside the pride
exercise: Why do lions roar? | To mark their territory and call their pride | To scare away flies | Because they are hungry and want food quickly | To call the rain
exercise: When do lions roar most? | At night and dawn, when sound carries best | Only at noon, when the sun is at its hottest | Only during thunderstorms | Only when they see people
exercise: Where do wild lions really live? | Savanna, grassland and open woodland in Africa, and Gir Forest in India | The thick, steamy jungles of South America and the Amazon rainforest | Rainforests of Borneo | Snowy forests of Siberia
exercise: Who does most of the hunting in a pride? | The lionesses, working as a team | The biggest male, alone | The cubs, for practice | All lions hunt alone
exercise: Why are lionesses the main hunters? | They are lighter and faster, and a team can catch big prey | Males do not like the taste of meat | Males cannot run at all | Lionesses can see in the dark and males cannot see at night at all
exercise: What is a pride made of? | Related females, their cubs and a few males | One big male and all of his grown-up sons | Only males who hunt together | A mother and one cub
exercise: Why is the lion called the only truly social cat? | It lives and hunts in a group, the pride | It likes to be stroked and fed by people in towns | It lives in towns with people | It shares food with other kinds of cats
exercise: Which country has three lions as its football symbol? | England | Brazil | Japan | Kenya
exercise: Who saves the lion in Aesop's fable? | A little mouse that gnaws through the net | A big elephant that tears the net | A wise old owl that calls the hunters away from the net | Another lion from the pride
exercise: What does simba mean in Swahili? | Lion | King | Brave | Sun
exercise: How long can a lion rest or sleep in a day? | Up to about 20 hours | Only up to about 2 hours | It never sleeps at all, day or night | Exactly 8 hours, like people
exercise: Why are lion cubs born with spotty coats? | Spots hide them in the grass | They catch a rash from the grass | Spots keep them warm on cold nights | To look like baby leopards to scare enemies
exercise: What is special about the end of a lion's tail? | It has a black tuft, the only cat with one | It has a sharp sting | It glows in the dark | It is long and bare like a rat's tail, with no hair
exercise: Where do Asiatic lions live today? | Only in and around Gir Forest in India | All across Europe | In the jungles of Borneo | In China and Japan
exercise: Why has the lion's range shrunk? | Farms and towns take its land, and lions are killed for attacking cattle | Lions moved to the jungle | Lions do not like the hot weather | Zebras and buffalo chased them away from their old hunting grounds
test: my poster says lion = king of the jungle. sounds right? | No, lions do not live in jungles; king of the beasts is better | Yes, all lions live in jungles | Yes, the thick jungle is their only real home and nowhere else | No, lions live in the sea
test: In The Lion King, Simba's dad does the hunting and the lionesses wait at home. What is real lion life like? | The lionesses do most of the hunting, the males guard the land | Males do all the hunting alone | Nobody hunts, the pride eats fruit and grass that grows near the den | The cubs hunt for the adults
test: A pride has 6 lionesses, 2 males and 5 cubs. How many manes can you count? | 2 | 8 | 13 | 0
test: same pride, 6 lionesses 2 males 5 cubs. how many legs then | 52 | 26 | 40 | 13
test: Ella's little brother says 4 lions have 32 ears between them. How many really? | 8, two each | 32, eight each | 4, one each | 16, four each
test: Jack says lions are lazy because they sleep all day. What is a better explanation? | They save energy for short bursts of hunting and avoid the midday heat | They are always ill | They are bored because there is nothing left for them to hunt anywhere | They are kings and do not have to work
test: Why does a lion roar at dawn rather than in the hot afternoon? | Sound carries furthest in cool, still air | Lions lose their voice in the hot afternoon | They need the sun to see who else is roaring | Dawn is the only time other lions are awake
test: What would happen to cubs if their mother went hunting and they had plain bright coats? | They would be easier for hyenas and other killers to spot | Nothing would happen, because colour does not matter to hyenas at all | They would be warmer and safer | They would hunt better
test: In a cartoon, a lion lives alone in a cave and never meets other lions. What is wrong? | Lions live in prides; they are the only truly social cats | Nothing, lions always live alone | Lions live in trees, not caves | Lions live together in big underground towns that they dig in the sand
test: why is it called Richard the Lionheart, did he have a lion's heart | No, it was a nickname for his bravery | Yes, a real lion's heart was put into him | Yes, he kept a real lion as a pet at his castle | No, it was the name of his favourite horse
test: A map shows lions once lived in southern Europe and much of Asia. What is the situation now? | Mainly parts of Africa, and Asiatic lions only around Gir Forest in India | Lions now live in every country | Lions have spread back into most of Europe and Asia, as they were before | Lions are now extinct everywhere
test: A tourist sees a hard little spur hidden in a lion's tail tuft. What is it for? | Nobody knows yet | It is a poison sting | It is used to dig holes | It holds the tuft on
exam: Identify the members of the pride by whom the greater part of the hunting is undertaken. | The lionesses, acting in concert as a team | The adult males, acting individually | The cubs, under the supervision of the males | Every member hunts independently and in isolation
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the habitat of the wild lion? | Savanna, grassland and open woodland in Africa, together with the Gir Forest of India | Dense tropical jungle throughout South America, together with the forests of Borneo | The coniferous forests of the far north | The ice fields of the Arctic
exam: Ascertain the approximate distance at which the roar of a lion may be heard. | Approximately eight kilometres | Approximately eighty metres | Approximately eighty kilometres | It is audible only within the immediate pride
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the social organisation of lions. | Lions are the only truly social cats, living in prides of related females with cubs and a few males | Lions live solitary lives and meet only to fight | Lions form great herds that migrate with the zebras across the plains each year, led by one male | Lions live in pairs that remain together for life
